// --no-sysroot-includes (tests/testnosysrootinc.flags): the toolchain-only view
// of the system include table — the C++ stdlib dirs and the compiler's builtin
// dir stay, the host's C library / SDK dirs do not. <zstd.h> is an add-on
// library header (libzstd-dev, a madc build dependency) that lives only in the
// sysroot's /usr/include and that no pack carries (a pack holds the C library,
// kernel and C++ standard library headers alone, served past the cut as
// madc's own), so this #include must fail LOUDLY (compile refused, the name in
// the diagnostic) on a packed binary and a pack-less one alike. Positive
// control: tests/testsysrootinc.mad, the same program without the flag. This
// is the FREEZER's posture (scripts/forest_pack_darwin.sh): a frozen forest is
// the header-less consumer's corpus, and a build host that carries a real SDK
// must not leak its text into the groves.
// The always-available println intrinsic keeps the program include-free apart
// from the sysroot-only header.
#include <zstd.h>
int main()
{
	int ok = ZSTD_VERSION_MAJOR >= 1;
	println("sysroot header resolved: {}", ok);
	return 0;
}
